 "location": {
  "name": "Plitvice Lakes National Park",
  "wiki": "Plitvice_Lakes_National_Park",
  "country": "Croatia",
  "region": "Lika-Senj and Karlovac counties, central Croatia",
  "lat": 44.8806,
  "lon": 15.6161,
  "tagline": "Sixteen turquoise lakes stacked down a limestone valley, joined by waterfalls that build themselves.",
  "intro": "Plitvice is Croatia's oldest and largest national park, a forested karst valley where sixteen terraced lakes spill into one another over natural travertine dams. Wooden boardwalks thread right across the water and past dozens of falls, ending at the 78-metre Veliki slap. It became one of the first UNESCO World Heritage Sites in 1979 and now draws about 1.5 million visitors a year.",
  "stats": [
   {
    "label": "National park since",
    "value": "1949"
   },
   {
    "label": "UNESCO World Heritage",
    "value": "Since 1979"
   },
   {
    "label": "Area",
    "value": "296.85 km²"
   },
   {
    "label": "Tallest waterfall",
    "value": "Veliki slap, 78 m"
   }
  ],
  "facts": [
   "The lake dams are alive in a sense: calcium carbonate from the water settles on mosses and hardens into travertine, and the barriers at Lake Kozjak grow by up to 13 mm a year.",
   "In March 1991 the park was the scene of the first deadly armed clash of the Croatian War of Independence, and its hotels were later used as barracks.",
   "Because of the war, UNESCO kept Plitvice on its List of World Heritage in Danger from 1992 to 1997.",
   "Between 1962 and 1968 it stood in for the American Wild West in German Karl May films — the lake Kaluđerovac played the 'Silver Lake' in Treasure of Silver Lake.",
   "Brown bears, grey wolves and lynx roam the park's forests, yet swimming in the lakes has been strictly banned since 2006."
  ],
  "bestTime": "Late spring brings the strongest waterfalls after the snowmelt, and October's fall colors are spectacular with thinner crowds; July and August are the busiest months.",
  "gettingThere": "Fly from New York to Zagreb (usually with one connection), then take a bus from Zagreb's main bus station to the park — about 2 hours 20 minutes — or rent a car for the roughly 140 km drive. Zadar, about 130 km away, is technically the closest airport.",
  "tip": "Buses stop outside both Entrance 1 and Entrance 2 with no real terminal, so check the posted return times at the wooden huts by the entrance as soon as you arrive."
 },
